GR-DOS: COMMAND.COM marks its batch segment as MS-DOS 6.22 does (issue #106).

MS-DOS 6.22's MOVE replaces files without asking when it is run from a
batch file. It is an external program: it finds COMMAND.COM's batch
segment by the bytes "cox" that 6.22's COMMAND.COM puts before it, in the
first 4 KB of the primary shell. COMMAND now has them too, so GR-DOS's
MOVE.EXE and Microsoft's both see a batch file, and ask at the prompt.

6.22's MOVE does not ask under COMMAND /C either, where 4.00's COMMAND
looks like a primary shell. COMMAND also marks its SINGLECOM word, nonzero
while a /C command runs, by "grdos/c", for GR-DOS's MOVE to see.

Applied to a copy of v4.0/src before it is converted to CRLF (Makefile,
PATCHES). `make check` builds without it.

--- a/CMD/COMMAND/RDATA.ASM
+++ b/CMD/COMMAND/RDATA.ASM
@@ -440,6 +440,13 @@
 ERRCD_24	DW	?
 HANDLE01	DW	?
 LOADING 	DB	0
+;
+; GR-DOS: MS-DOS 6.22's mark of the batch segment, the word that follows
+; it.  MOVE (and Microsoft's own MOVE.EXE) finds it in the first 4 KB of
+; the primary COMMAND.COM to tell a command typed at the prompt from one
+; in a batch file, which replaces files without asking (issue #106).
+;
+BatchMark	DB	"cox"
 BATCH		DW	0			; Assume no batch mode initially
 COMSPEC 	DB	64 DUP(0)
 comspec_end	dw	?
@@ -468,6 +475,12 @@
 IO_SAVE 	DW	?
 RESTDIR 	DB	0
 PERMCOM 	DB	0			; TRUE => permanent command
+;
+; GR-DOS: the mark of SINGLECOM, the word that follows it, nonzero while a
+; COMMAND /C command runs.  MOVE finds it as it finds BatchMark, and does
+; not ask under COMMAND /C, as MS-DOS 6.22's does not (issue #106).
+;
+SingleMark	DB	"grdos/c"
 SINGLECOM	DW	0			; TRUE => single command version
 VERVAL		DW	-1
 fFail		DB	0			; TRUE => FAIL all INT 24s
